# Ptyxis for MIU Darwin

Ptyxis is a modern terminal emulator for GNOME, built on GTK4 and libadwaita.
This port targets MIU Darwin (Darwin/XNU, x86_64), cross compiled from Fedora.

## Building

MIU Darwin has no ports tree.  The equivalent of a port Makefile is a pair of
shell scripts kept in the MIU Darwin tree:

| OpenBSD ports        | MIU Darwin                       |
|----------------------|----------------------------------|
| `Makefile`           | `userland/ptyxis/build.sh`       |
| `make install`       | `scripts/deploy-ptyxis.sh`       |
| `WANTLIB`            | dependency check inside deploy    |
| `distinfo`           | `distinfo` (hex SHA256, see below)|

### Direct meson build

Everything is cross compiled, so the meson cross file carries the compiler,
the sysroot and the linker flags.  See `miu-cross.ini` in the MIU Darwin tree.

```sh
curl -O https://download.gnome.org/sources/ptyxis/50/ptyxis-50.1.tar.xz
tar xf ptyxis-50.1.tar.xz
cd ptyxis-50.1

patch -p0 < /path/to/patches/patch-agent_meson_build
patch -p0 < /path/to/patches/patch-src_ptyxis-tab_c

meson setup build --cross-file /path/to/miu-cross.ini \
      --prefix=/usr --libdir=lib
ninja -C build
DESTDIR=$PWD/stage meson install -C build --no-rebuild
```

### Installing into the image

MIU Darwin runs from a 256 MB disk image, so installation means copying into
that image rather than into a live filesystem:

```sh
scripts/deploy-ptyxis.sh
```

That script copies the binaries, the dylibs Ptyxis needs (libvte, liblz4,
libc++), the desktop file, the icons and the Japanese translation, then
recompiles the GSettings schemas.  It refuses to write anything if a dylib
Ptyxis links against is missing from the image, checked with otool.

## distinfo

The checksum is written as **hex**, not the base64 form OpenBSD's `distinfo`
uses.  MIU Darwin's build scripts verify with `sha256sum -c`, which expects
hex, and a file nobody can check with the tools at hand is worse than no file.

## MIU Darwin-Specific Patches

### patch-agent_meson_build

`agent/meson.build` includes `x86_64/force_link_glibc_2.17.h` for every
x86_64 build, regardless of the operating system.  MIU Darwin has its own C
library and no glibc symbol versioning, so the agent fails to build.

The condition is written against `host_machine`, not `target_machine`: in
meson, `target_machine` only carries a meaning when the artefact being built
is itself a compiler.  For an application, the machine the code runs on is
`host_machine`.  The distinction is not academic here, because the build is a
cross compile.

The OpenBSD port carries the same fix (written against `target_machine`,
which is equivalent for a native build).

### patch-src_ptyxis-tab_c

`src/ptyxis-tab.c` uses `WIFEXITED`, `WEXITSTATUS`, `WIFSIGNALED` and
`WTERMSIG` without including `<sys/wait.h>`.  POSIX places those macros in
that header; the file builds on glibc only because another header happens to
pull it in.

The OpenBSD port carries the same fix.  Two ports hitting the same omission
independently is a fair sign the include is simply missing upstream, so this
one is also queued for submission (`upstream-patches/`).

## Patches that were *not* needed

### wordexp(3)

The OpenBSD port disables `wordexp()` in `ptyxis_path_expand()`, because
OpenBSD does not provide it.  MIU Darwin implements `wordexp(3)` in its own C
library, so no patch is required here and the function takes its normal path.

The implementation deliberately returns an error rather than silently
ignoring what it cannot expand (pathname expansion, command substitution),
which matches what Ptyxis expects: it falls back to a copy of the input when
`wordexp()` fails.

### pledge(2) / unveil(2)

OpenBSD-specific sandboxing.  MIU Darwin has no equivalent yet.

## Not available on MIU Darwin

| Feature                          | Reason                          |
|----------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| Podman/Toolbox/Distrobox         | Linux container runtimes        |
| libportal                        | Flatpak portals                 |
| systemd scopes                   | systemd                         |
| GPU rendering                    | cairo renderer only, no GL yet  |
